{
  "dom_id": "step-q362882",
  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=\\frac{4}{t}$, where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=-8\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=1\\,\\text{s}$, determine the moment in time when the particle is stationary. Do not round your answer.\n\nThe particle is stationary at $t=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,\\text{s}.$",
  "worked_solution": "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{4}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(t)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=-8.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8 & =4\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(1)+C \\\\ -8 & =0+C \\\\ C & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=4\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(t)-8.$ \n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(t)-8 & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061(t) & =2 \\\\ t & ={e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary when $t={e}^{2}\\,\\text{s}.$",
